00:00:18.572 --> 00:00:20.380
VAIBHAV SINGH: And
today we're going

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.590
to talk about PyTorch
at scale on cloud TPUs.

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:27.600
So let's get started.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.850
And here is going to be
our map for the talk.

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:32.189
We're going to start
with an introduction.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:34.160
Then we'll move to a
step-by-step walkthrough

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.270
of an example.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:39.240
We will discuss how
it exactly works,

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.880
and how to scale your PyTorch
training on Cloud TPUs.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.350
And finally, we are going to
leave you with resources, tips,

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.750
and tricks so that you can
start to train your PyTorch

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.840
model on TPUs today.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.720
PyTorch is officially
supported on Cloud TPUs.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.750
This support is made possible
through the Torch XLA

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:00.150
library, an open
source initiative

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:03.070
from Facebook and Google.

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:06.730
It was announced in October
2019 at PyTorch DevCon

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:08.950
and has been growing ever since.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:12.400
The support reached beta
with PyTorch 1.5 release

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.830
and would be generally
available by the time you'll

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:16.210
be seeing this recording.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:19.760
For those of you who are
not very familiar with TPUs,

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:23.230
TPUs, or Tensor Processing
Unit, is a hardware accelerator

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:26.170
built by Google in
2015, first to address

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:27.880
its own internal needs.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:29.920
The very first
generation of TPUs

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.080
was an inference-only chip.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:33.970
Beginning with
version 2, it evolved

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:36.070
to support training as well.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:37.870
And beginning with
version 2, Google also

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.420
made TPUs available to
the rest of the world

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.700
through Google Cloud,
with the vision

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:46.330
to help push machine learning
state of the art forward.

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:49.150
You can scale your
machine learning workloads

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:53.380
using TPUs from eight cores
all the way to TPU pods.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:56.500
TPU pods consist of
thousands of TPU cores.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:59.200
As you can see on the
left-hand side of your screen,

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.540
TPU comes in two
different flavors

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:03.520
on Google Cloud as of today.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.460
That is TPU v2 and v3

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.860
You can start with
a device, as you

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.980
can see on the left-hand top
corner of your screen, all

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:14.060
the way to the full pod.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:17.580
Our Cloud TPU v2 pod
comes with 512 cores,

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.480
and our Cloud TPU v3 pod
comes the 2,048 cores.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.130
You can choose a slice
of a pod, all the way

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:28.250
to the full pod for your
machine learning trainings.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:31.010
Here is a Cloud TPU device.

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:33.530
In order to access
this device, a user

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:36.980
creates a VM, or a Virtual
Machine, on Google Cloud

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.850
and creates an
instance of Cloud TPU.

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:42.290
You can create this instance
in different device types.

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:45.870
In this case, we are
choosing v3-8 That

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:48.170
indicates a v3 device.

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:50.720
Each v3-8 comes with four chips.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:56.090
Each of these chips has got two
cores, and each of these cores

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:58.700
come equipped with
matrix multiplication

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:01.520
units, two matrix
multiplication units each,

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:05.630
and 16 GB of high
bandwidth memory per core.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:08.120
All in all, this device
has got eight cores,

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.190
and that's why when you
choose the accelerator

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:12.020
type when you are
creating this device,

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:14.930
you choose v3-8 or v2-8.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:19.750
Whereas when you're working with
pod, in case of a Cloud TPU v3,

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:25.550
a full TPU pod consists of 256
TPU devices, or 2,048 cores,

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:28.010
interconnected with the
high speed interconnect,

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:32.840
which lets the
gradient averaging,

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:36.170
or all-reduce operation,
run very efficiently

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:38.180
on the full pod.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:41.840
The TPU software stack enables
you to train all the way

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:45.350
from a TPU device to the TPU
pod or any intermediate size

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:47.270
without any code changes.

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:50.270
And it always acts
as a single node

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:52.640
without any complicated
cluster setup.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:57.530
Now let's take a look into
an example of a PyTorch code

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:02.643
as we know it, and then see how
this code needs to be modified

00:04:02.643 --> 00:04:04.310
or what other
modifications we typically

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:08.120
see in order to run that
PyTorch code on Cloud TPUs.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.520
So first of all, let's begin
with a familiar territory.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:13.970
For those of you who are
familiar with distributed

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:16.790
training on PyTorch,
you have already

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:24.380
seen similar examples, similar
elements such as the PyTorch

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:26.270
distributed libraries.

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:30.170
You see the model, you see
the model being transferred

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:34.250
to the devices, in
this case GPU devices,

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:36.800
you see the familiar
elements like DataLoader

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.670
and DistributedSampler.

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:40.820
The DistributedSampler
makes sure that each

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:42.980
of the replicas of
the model are actually

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:45.170
looking at the different
slices of the data,

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:48.620
not looking at the same slice.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:55.410
You see familiar elements
like the train DataLoader.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:58.830
You see the forward pass
within the training loop

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:02.070
and the backward pass,
which computes the gradient.

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:04.380
You see the Optimizer step.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:07.350
That is going to
update the parameter

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:11.490
based on the computed gradient
and the Optimizer of choice.

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.950
And you notice the
Torch multiprocessing

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:18.000
spawn method, which
executes this training loop.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.910
In this particular example,
four different replicas

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:24.900
of this model are
running in parallel.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:28.320
Now let's see how
this code changes

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:32.460
when we are going to train
it on Cloud TPU device.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.770
As I was describing,
a Cloud TPU device

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:39.660
consists of four Cloud TPU
chips on eight TPU cores.

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:44.170
Beginning with the elements
like Torch XLA library,

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:47.880
this is what makes the training
of PyTorch model on TPU

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.380
possible.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:53.200
Similar to what you
saw in case of GPU, now

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.590
we have another device
abstraction called XLA device.

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:59.010
This is an abstraction
of Cloud TPU core.

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:02.250
Similar to how you were
transferring your model

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:07.980
on Cloud TPU device, you
are transferring your model

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.080
on Cloud TPU core here.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:16.470
And finally, the elements
like the DistributedSampler

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:20.070
or the DataLoader remain exactly
the same with one difference,

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:22.380
that we are using a
parallel loader, which

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:26.160
is a wrapper, here that makes
sure that the data upload

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:30.180
operations to TPUs are run in
parallel with the computations

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:33.670
in order to make the
data pipeline efficient.

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:36.280
One more important
distinction to highlight here

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:39.220
is that we are using
the Optimizer step

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:44.740
method from XLA module instead
of optimizer.step method

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:47.590
that you saw in the
previous example.

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:50.890
This is to make sure that
the all-reduce operation is

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:56.410
performed first before the
optimizer.step is called

00:06:56.410 --> 00:07:00.800
to update the parameters.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:04.460
And finally, instead of the
Torch multiprocessing module,

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:07.940
you have XLA
multiprocessing module,

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:12.080
which has the similar spawn
method, which under the hood

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:14.420
takes care of all the
necessary TPU device

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:19.340
initialization and setup before
using the Torch multiprocessing

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:23.010
module to start again
in a similar fashion.

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:25.760
The process is
running in parallel

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:30.590
to facilitate the training
on eight different replica

00:07:30.590 --> 00:07:32.410
of your model in
this case, because we

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:34.400
are running on eight cores.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:36.980
And also notice the start
method equal to fork,

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:41.060
which is used to make sure
that the model is copied

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:42.380
into the memory only once.

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:44.300
Each of the child
process will be

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:48.740
able to use all the resources
that spun up before that

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:51.120
in the global scope.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:57.310
Now let's look at a live
example of one of the trainings

00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:01.180
that we are going to do
on ResNet-18 model using

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:04.443
the CIFAR10 data set.

00:08:04.443 --> 00:08:05.860
So I start with
the Cloud console.

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:17.750
Here I have set up a
Cloud AtPlatform notebook

00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:20.540
instance with
PyTorch XLA library

00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:23.850
and all the necessary
components ready to go.

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:27.980
So I start my notebook instance.

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:32.864
In the meantime, notice also
that I have created a Cloud TPU

00:08:32.864 --> 00:08:33.364
instance.

00:08:37.929 --> 00:08:40.390
That's a v3-8 device.

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:43.059
So four chips, eight cores.

00:08:43.059 --> 00:08:44.740
Notice the runtime
version that is

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:47.540
displayed in this column
whenever you create a Cloud TPU

