00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:43.710
Now that you're familiar
with Torch XLA usage,

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:47.240
you may be wondering
how exactly it works.

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:51.350
If you're familiar with
PyTorch tensor class or ATen,

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:54.620
you already know that it extends
support to multiple device

00:11:54.620 --> 00:11:58.670
implementations or memory layout
through dynamic dispatches

00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:00.740
to those implementations.

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:03.920
Torch XLA extends ATen
with a new type of tensor

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:05.790
called XLA tensor.

00:12:05.790 --> 00:12:11.060
XLA tensor is different from
other eager implementations

00:12:11.060 --> 00:12:14.490
in that it's a lazy tensor.

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:18.180
While eager tensors are
dispatched op by op,

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:20.820
and the only computation
graph that is generated

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:23.280
is for the gradient
computation, and as soon

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:26.040
as the gradient computation
or the backward pass is done,

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:30.640
this graph is destroyed,
in case of lazy tensor,

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:34.950
PyTorch XLA just constructs an
XLA intermediate representation

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:38.400
graph with no
evaluation is performed

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:43.020
on this graph until required,
either by a certain operation

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:46.440
in your model code
itself, or by a call that

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:51.250
necessitate the context transfer
from the tensor to scalar.

00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:55.170
Building this IR graph also
enables PyTorch XLA to do

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:57.155
certain graph-level
optimizations,

00:12:57.155 --> 00:12:58.530
which are not
possible if you are

00:12:58.530 --> 00:13:01.200
doing the op by op dispatch.

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:05.280
This IR graph is then
compiled and sent

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:07.950
to TPU device for execution.

00:13:07.950 --> 00:13:11.820
Between training steps, if
the IR graphs are identical,

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:13.590
XLA compilation
is not performed.

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:17.190
However, if IR graph is going
to change too frequently

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:20.700
between training step or because
of the nature of the code

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:24.060
itself, every time it
changes, the XLA compilation

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:25.560
needs to be performed.

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:27.810
And it might slow
down your training.

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:32.340
To take more details into what
are the things to watch out for

00:13:32.340 --> 00:13:36.390
and how you can make the most
of your PyTorch training,

00:13:36.390 --> 00:13:38.490
here is my colleague
Taylan, who will

00:13:38.490 --> 00:13:41.310
talk about tips and tricks.

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:42.310
TAYLAN BILAL: All right.

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:44.350
Thanks Vaibhav.

00:13:44.350 --> 00:13:49.660
So let me talk about some
of the tips and tricks,

00:13:49.660 --> 00:13:55.170
things to watch out for when you
port your model from GPU code

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:57.250
to TPU code.

00:13:57.250 --> 00:14:02.200
One of the most common pitfalls
is dynamic input shapes.

00:14:02.200 --> 00:14:05.080
As Vaibhav noted
earlier, every time

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:09.190
the intermediate
representation graph changes,

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:12.120
this triggers an
XLA recompilation,

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:14.150
and that slows things down.

00:14:14.150 --> 00:14:19.360
And this happens when the model
has input shapes that change

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:22.720
from one step to another.

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:25.090
A good example is
an NLP model where

00:14:25.090 --> 00:14:29.540
you have sentences of
varying length, for example,

00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:33.520
or an image model with
images changing shapes.

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:37.330
Another common pitfall
is what we call a context

00:14:37.330 --> 00:14:40.340
change between device to CPU.

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:46.210
And the most common occurrence
for that is when the code has

00:14:46.210 --> 00:14:49.210
tensor .item calls in it.

00:14:49.210 --> 00:14:52.820
This slows things down
because, as Vaibhav

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:59.260
noted earlier, XLA is built
on a lazy tensor idea,

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:04.300
and the item calls
prematurely causes evaluation,

00:15:04.300 --> 00:15:09.400
and the result is
suboptimal if that happens.

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:13.530
Third most common pitfall
is if the model code

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:19.990
is using some operation that
has no XLA implementation yet.

00:15:19.990 --> 00:15:23.050
Then that doesn't
cause an error,

00:15:23.050 --> 00:15:25.390
but it causes the
IR graph to get cut,

00:15:25.390 --> 00:15:29.680
and the operation that's
not lowered to XLA

00:15:29.680 --> 00:15:33.290
gets executed on the CPU,
which slows things down.

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:36.520
We want to stay in the device
context as much as possible.

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:40.580
When that happens, these things
happen, we have solutions.

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:43.730
For example, for the first
problem, dynamic input shapes,

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:45.490
we may use padding.

00:15:45.490 --> 00:15:48.790
We may take the sentences
with varying length

00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:51.700
and pad it to a fixed length.

00:15:51.700 --> 00:15:55.390
Or maybe we predetermine
three or four fixed lengths

00:15:55.390 --> 00:15:58.420
and pad it to the next length.

00:15:58.420 --> 00:16:02.290
And in the latter case, we
will still have recompilations,

00:16:02.290 --> 00:16:04.450
because the input
shapes will take

00:16:04.450 --> 00:16:06.470
three or four different values.

00:16:06.470 --> 00:16:11.170
And the first time they are
seen, we're going to compile.

00:16:11.170 --> 00:16:13.720
But we're going to
waste less FLOPs

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:15.970
as a result of less padding.

00:16:15.970 --> 00:16:17.950
So there is a trade-off there.

00:16:17.950 --> 00:16:21.130
Second, when we have the tensor
to scalar context change,

00:16:21.130 --> 00:16:24.430
or device to CPU
context change, this

00:16:24.430 --> 00:16:29.350
can happen because of, for
example, loss reporting,

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:31.840
because loss is
usually a tensor.

00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:34.810
What we do, we recommend
to reduce the item calls,

00:16:34.810 --> 00:16:40.300
and report loss every n steps
instead of maybe every step.

00:16:40.300 --> 00:16:43.060
n can take a value
of 20, 30, 100.

00:16:43.060 --> 00:16:45.790
That's up to the user.

00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:48.280
The user could also
have item calls

00:16:48.280 --> 00:16:51.580
from another part of the model.

00:16:51.580 --> 00:16:54.410
They need to be
spotted and eliminated.

00:16:54.410 --> 00:16:58.220
In the third case, if we
encounter an op with no XLA

00:16:58.220 --> 00:17:02.900
lowering, we usually just
create a GitHub issue

00:17:02.900 --> 00:17:09.859
in github.com/pytorch/xla and
ask the Torch XLA team to lower

00:17:09.859 --> 00:17:14.270
the said op so that the
operation runs on the device

00:17:14.270 --> 00:17:16.800
and is accelerated as a result.

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:20.660
We have talked about
problems and solutions,

00:17:20.660 --> 00:17:23.760
but how do you actually
understand what is going on

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:25.710
and what the
problem actually is?

00:17:25.710 --> 00:17:27.810
So this is the
slide from earlier.

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:31.730
We want to focus on the
white rectangles here.

00:17:31.730 --> 00:17:35.190
We are importing Torch
XLA debug metrics here.

00:17:35.190 --> 00:17:37.130
And we are printing
the metrics report.

00:17:37.130 --> 00:17:40.010
This metrics report contains a
lot of information about what's

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:41.720
going on at the XLA level.

00:17:41.720 --> 00:17:43.760
And it can surface
some of the issues

00:17:43.760 --> 00:17:46.670
that I just discussed
in the previous slides.

00:17:46.670 --> 00:17:49.230
And let's look at an
example metrics report.

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:52.670
This is just a summary
that focuses on the issues

00:17:52.670 --> 00:17:54.260
that we discussed.

00:17:54.260 --> 00:17:56.840
The actual metrics report
contains more information

00:17:56.840 --> 00:17:58.430
than just this.

00:17:58.430 --> 00:18:03.510
If you look at the first
part, the metric compile time,

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:06.470
this gives us information about
how many graph recompilations

00:18:06.470 --> 00:18:08.990
have occurred in this workload.

00:18:08.990 --> 00:18:15.260
And in this case, we have 202
compilations that occurred.

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:18.080
And the total time
it took is 6 minutes.

00:18:18.080 --> 00:18:20.480
Now the metrics
report is usually

00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:23.280
composed of metrics
and the counters.

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:25.820
The counters give
information about what's

00:18:25.820 --> 00:18:27.260
happening in the code.

00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:29.840
For example, the
Counter MarkStep

00:18:29.840 --> 00:18:37.080
tells you how many training
steps that we have taken.

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:40.880
For example, in this case,
232 steps have been taken.

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:44.150
And that resulted
in 202 compilations,

00:18:44.150 --> 00:18:49.070
which suggests that there
is a problem with the graphs

00:18:49.070 --> 00:18:50.620
that the code is generating.

00:18:50.620 --> 00:18:54.140
The compilation should be low.

00:18:54.140 --> 00:18:56.540
Some compilations
initially are OK,

00:18:56.540 --> 00:19:00.350
but it shouldn't grow as
the number of steps grow.

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:04.670
Similarly, for example,
in the item call problem,

00:19:04.670 --> 00:19:06.770
the context switch
between device and CPU,

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:09.370
the counter ATen
local_scalar_dense

00:19:09.370 --> 00:19:10.600
will be your friend.

00:19:10.600 --> 00:19:14.630
And here in this case, you
have 240 occurrences of that.

00:19:14.630 --> 00:19:18.620
And that's something
that needs to be reduced

00:19:18.620 --> 00:19:20.660
to get optimal performance.

00:19:20.660 --> 00:19:22.790
In the third problem
that we discussed,

00:19:22.790 --> 00:19:24.560
the op that's not
been lowered yet,

00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:29.450
you will see an ATen counter
with the op's actual name.

00:19:29.450 --> 00:19:34.580
And in this case, we
have 232, probably once

00:19:34.580 --> 00:19:39.830
per step, some op that
needs to be lowered in XLA.

00:19:39.830 --> 00:19:42.900
And once you iron
those issues out,

00:19:42.900 --> 00:19:47.180
the code will run much faster
and will be very performance.

00:19:47.180 --> 00:19:49.250
Now so far, we have
seen how training

00:19:49.250 --> 00:19:51.480
works on a Cloud TPU device.

00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:55.820
Now let's discuss how to
take it to the next level

00:19:55.820 --> 00:19:57.920
and train on a Cloud TPU pod.

00:19:57.920 --> 00:20:00.950
As pods were described
by Vaibhav earlier,

00:20:00.950 --> 00:20:04.880
they consist of several
devices connected

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:06.570
with very fast network.

00:20:06.570 --> 00:20:10.430
And in the PyTorch
XLA context, we

00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:16.920
create as many user VMs as the
Cloud TPU devices, not cores.

00:20:16.920 --> 00:20:21.590
So for example, if you want
to train on a v3-32, 32 cores,

00:20:21.590 --> 00:20:25.490
we would need to create four
VMs to drive the training.

00:20:25.490 --> 00:20:27.960
And we wrap these user
VMs in an instance group.

00:20:27.960 --> 00:20:30.630
So everything needs to
be in an instance group.

00:20:30.630 --> 00:20:34.280
And once we have set
up these resources,

00:20:34.280 --> 00:20:38.580
launching the training
distributed mode is very easy.

00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:41.180
Now let's look at the
command to do that.

00:20:41.180 --> 00:20:44.450
Here, the white part
of the large command

00:20:44.450 --> 00:20:46.610
is the actual
command that you were

00:20:46.610 --> 00:20:48.840
running to run on one device.

00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:51.440
So this is your
code with arguments.

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:56.180
And all you need to do to
scale it up to a pod level

00:20:56.180 --> 00:21:00.480
is just prefix the command
with these elements.

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:04.900
And here, the green
part is xla_dist.

00:21:04.900 --> 00:21:06.980
It's offered by Torch XLA.

00:21:06.980 --> 00:21:10.820
And it coordinates
the resources,

00:21:10.820 --> 00:21:14.690
sets up the mesh between the
instance group and the TPUs,

00:21:14.690 --> 00:21:16.760
and sets up the environment
variables specified

00:21:16.760 --> 00:21:20.690
by the next set of elements.

00:21:20.690 --> 00:21:22.850
For example, the TPU name,
the conda environment,

00:21:22.850 --> 00:21:24.410
and any other
environment variable

00:21:24.410 --> 00:21:26.580
that you might want to set up.

00:21:26.580 --> 00:21:28.400
And that's it.

00:21:28.400 --> 00:21:30.675
There's really no code changes.

00:21:30.675 --> 00:21:33.440
One gotcha here
is the code needs

00:21:33.440 --> 00:21:35.510
to be written so
that the data is

00:21:35.510 --> 00:21:38.000
charted across all the cores.

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:44.030
Otherwise the VMs will end
up doing the same work,

00:21:44.030 --> 00:21:45.070
unnecessary work.

00:21:45.070 --> 00:21:46.580
But that's really
the only gotcha,

00:21:46.580 --> 00:21:51.470
and scaling up is as easy as
just prefixing your command.

00:21:51.470 --> 00:21:55.240
So now let's look at some of
the models that actually have

00:21:55.240 --> 00:21:58.290
been tested and known to work.

00:21:58.290 --> 00:22:01.570
In the computer
vision area, we have

00:22:01.570 --> 00:22:03.730
ResNet and ResNeXt, along
with most TorchVision

00:22:03.730 --> 00:22:07.540
models such as MobileNet and
others that already work.

00:22:07.540 --> 00:22:10.540
In the NLP area, we have
transformers that work.

00:22:10.540 --> 00:22:13.270
For example, FairSeq's
Transformer, RoBERTa,

00:22:13.270 --> 00:22:15.190
or HuggingFace's Transformers.

00:22:15.190 --> 00:22:19.390
We have a working implementation
of a deep convolutional again.

00:22:19.390 --> 00:22:21.730
That's up on our GitHub.

00:22:21.730 --> 00:22:23.920
And in the speech
recognition world,

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:28.890
we have worked with FairSeq's
Wave2Vec and vector quantized

00:22:28.890 --> 00:22:29.920
Wave2Vec.

00:22:29.920 --> 00:22:32.530
Let me note here that the
models that work on Torch XLA

00:22:32.530 --> 00:22:33.700
aren't limited to these.

00:22:33.700 --> 00:22:37.420
These have been
tested, but people

00:22:37.420 --> 00:22:41.110
have run other workloads
and other models

00:22:41.110 --> 00:22:42.950
successfully as well.

00:22:42.950 --> 00:22:45.440
And you can port
your model as well.

00:22:45.440 --> 00:22:47.700
To get started, here
are some resources.

00:22:47.700 --> 00:22:51.730
So these are some of the
tutorials that are offered

00:22:51.730 --> 00:22:53.830
on Google Cloud web pages.

00:22:53.830 --> 00:22:56.500
And it covers some of the
models that I just discussed

00:22:56.500 --> 00:22:58.620
in the previous slide.

00:22:58.620 --> 00:23:01.320
And along with the
Google Cloud tutorials,

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:05.130
there are Kaggle kernels from
some of the Kaggle experts

00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:07.920
in the community.

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:12.090
They have successfully run
competitions on Torch XLA,

00:23:12.090 --> 00:23:15.740
and they offer their
kernels to the users.

00:23:15.740 --> 00:23:18.115
And finally, here is a summary
slide of all the resources

00:23:18.115 --> 00:23:18.615
that we saw.

00:23:21.400 --> 00:23:23.020
Some of the GitHub
repositories that

00:23:23.020 --> 00:23:25.330
are relevant to the project,
official tutorials, Kaggle

00:23:25.330 --> 00:23:26.500
kernels.

00:23:26.500 --> 00:23:28.690
And please visit PyTorch XLA.

00:23:28.690 --> 00:23:34.900
It's a very active open source
library, and open issues

00:23:34.900 --> 00:23:37.900
if you encounter any
issues, et cetera.

00:23:37.900 --> 00:23:40.110
Thanks for listening.

